('wger_1510','Neutral Grip Lat Pulldown','Neutral Grip Lat Pulldown','Lat pull downs with a neutral grip on the bar.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1510','CC-BY-SA 4','Expenses7000','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1511','Kong Curl','Kong Curl','This exercise is somewhat similar to a cable curl and a cable crossover. But instead of a chest/biceps exercise, this exercise targets the brachialis muscle, responsible for that broad arm when viewed from a front view.
During the exercise, maintain a pronated forearm position with starting position of approx. 45 degree pronated. Start with slightly bend elbows and alternately curl until your hands (your thumb first) meet your upper middle chest (similar to how Kong hits his chest, thus the name of this exercise).','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['biceps']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1511','CC-BY-SA 4','Moffi','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1512','Drop Curl','Drop Curl','Drop curls in a modified position at a slow pace effectively engage the brachialis for arm width.
Starting position is with both arms in a slight angle towards each other at chest height with a dumbbell in each hand. Lower one dumbbell close to full extended arm position slowly and in a controlled manner during the first half of the curl, then curl it back up to starting position, while the other dumbbell stays at its starting position. Now lower the other dumbbell down. This keeps tension on the brachialis. As we pass the 90deg mark on our way down, the biceps will take over the work.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['biceps']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1512','CC-BY-SA 4','Moffi','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1513','Overhead Cable Tricep Extension','Overhead Cable Tricep Extension','Use rope handle with your back against the cable machine. Straighten your arms until they are filly extended and reverse the motion resisting on the negative.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['triceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1513','CC-BY-SA 4','Expenses7000','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1514','Lateral Walk','Lateral Walk','Lateral walks, also known as side steps or squat walks, are a type of exercise where you move sideways in a squatting position. They can be performed with or without resistance bands. These exercises strengthen the hip abductors, glutes, and other stabilizing muscles.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['glutes']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1514','CC-BY-SA 4','cozyGalvinism','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1515','Leg Press Toe Press','Leg Press Toe Press','Move the leg press using your calves by placing your feet at the bottom of the platform.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['calves']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1515','CC-BY-SA 4','Expenses7000','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1516','Handstand','Handstand','Handstand free standing by pressing arms into the ground and contracting core and glutes','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1516','CC-BY-SA 4','Fittness69','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1519','Overhead Triceps Extension','Overhead Triceps Extension','Overhead Triceps Extension with EZ Bar – Quick Guide

The overhead position emphasizes the long head of the triceps (because it''s stretched under load), the exercise does engage all three triceps heads (long, lateral, and medial) to some degree during extension.

Setup:
Sit on a bench (or stand) and hold an EZ bar with a narrow, overhand
grip. Lift the bar overhead, arms fully extended. Keep your core tight
and elbows close to your head.
Execution:
Slowly lower the bar behind your head by bending your elbows until they
reach ~90°. Keep elbows pointed forward (not flaring out).
Finish: Extend your arms back up to the starting position, squeezing your triceps at the top.

Tips:

Control the movement to avoid straining your shoulders.
Use a moderate weight for full range of motion.
Keep your back straight and avoid arching.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1519/0.gif',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1519','CC-BY-SA 4','benjamin.yildiz@proton.me','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_152','Chin Up','Chin Up','The chin-up (also known as a chin or chinup) is a strength training exercise. People frequently do this exercise with the intention of strengthening muscles such as the latissimus dorsi and biceps, which extend the shoulder and flex the elbow, respectively. In this maneuver, the palms are faced towards the body. It is a form of pull-up in which the range of motion is established in relation to a person''s chin.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS','BACK']::text[],ARRAY['biceps','lats']::text[],ARRAY['PULL_UP_BAR']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_152/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','152','CC0','BFad07','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1521','Pendular hack','Pendular hack','Pendulum hack, made on a pendulum machine','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],'{}'::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1521/0.webp',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1521','CC-BY-SA 4','polloperro','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1523','Sled Push','Sled Push','Load the sled with 25% of your maximum load. If you don’t know this, choose a weight you can push for 10 minutes with short breaks. Beginners may choose to push the sled with no weight.
Stand behind the sled and grab the poles with a high-grip hand position.
Engage your core muscles and start pushing the sled forward as fast as you can, powering through your entire leg. Extend your hips and knees as you move the sled forward. Your foot stance should resemble your natural running position.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','SHOULDERS','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['calves','glutes','quads','shoulders','hamstrings','triceps']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1523','CC-BY-SA 4','flanny','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1524','Battle Ropes','Battle Ropes','Hold the ends of the rope at arm''s length in front of your hips with your hands shoulder-width apart.
Brace your core and begin alternately raising and lowering each arm explosively.
Keep alternating arms for three to four sets of 1 to 2 minutes.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','BACK','CORE','SHOULDERS','ARMS','CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes','lats','quads','core','shoulders','biceps','chest','triceps']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1524','CC-BY-SA 4','flanny','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1525','Ball Slams','Ball Slams','Stand with your feet about shoulder-width apart, your knees and hips slightly bent, holding the ball in both hands at chest height. Engage your core, and keep a good posture.
Extend your knees and drive your hips forward while simultaneously lifting the ball. Aim for being as tall as possible, the ball overhead, arms up, hips slightly forward, and on your toes from the force of your drive.
Use your core and arms to slam the medicine ball straight down between your feet with as much force as possible. Press your hips back and bend your knees to further power the slam. Exhale as you slam the ball down.
Squat down to pick up the ball from the floor, then immediately move into the next slam by repeating the movement. Repeat for reps or time.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK','CORE','SHOULDERS','LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['lats','core','shoulders','glutes']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1525','CC-BY-SA 4','flanny','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1526','Ski Machine','Ski Machine','Start standing on the platform with your feet hip-width apart.
Reach overhead to grip the handles with your palms facing in.
Soften your knees, then simultaneously drive your butt back as if you''re closing a door behind you while pulling your arms straight down past your hips until your hands pass by the side of your knees.
Next, bring your arms back overhead while thrusting your hips forward until you''re standing with your arms fully extended.
Repeat for reps, time or distance.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','BACK','ARMS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['calves','lats','triceps','hamstrings','glutes','quads','core']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1526','CC-BY-SA 4','flanny','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1527','Pendulum Squat','Pendulum Squat','Place your feet in the middle of the plate at about shoulder width. Tense your torso. Keep your neck relaxed.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['quads','hamstrings','glutes']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1527','CC-BY-SA 4','schweezer','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1528','Glute Drive','Glute Drive','Lie down on the back pad and strap yourself in with the waistband. Position yout feet shoulder-length apart. They should be slightly splayed.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['glutes','hamstrings','quads']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1528','CC-BY-SA 4','schweezer','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1529','Toes to bar','Toes to bar','The name "Toes to Bar" says it all: This exercise, in which you hang from a pull-up bar, involves bringing your toes toward the bar, pointing toward your face. This really works your abdominal muscles. "Toes to Bar" is popular in functional fitness programs like Freeletics and CrossFit, and is an effective exercise for six-pack training.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE','BACK']::text[],ARRAY['core','lats']::text[],ARRAY['PULL_UP_BAR']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1529','CC-BY-SA 4','florian.bussmann','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1530','Lying Dumbbell Curls','Lying Dumbbell Curls','Take a
dumbbell in each hand and lie backwards on a bench, similar to a classic chest press.
Let your arms hang down at your sides, with the dumbbells possibly touching the
floor. Now start to lift the dumbbells upwards until your forearms are
perpendicular to the ceiling. Slowly lower the dumbbells until they almost
touch the floor again. Keep your ellbows in place to minimize cheating.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['biceps']::text[],ARRAY['BENCH','DUMBBELL']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1530','CC-BY-SA 4','Moffi','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1531','Cable Curls','Cable Curls','Bicep Curls using cables. Can be seated or standing','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['biceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1531','CC-BY-SA 4','Brigade7938','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1536','Delt Stretch','Delt Stretch','Stretch your deltoids for a certain period of time.','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1536','CC-BY-SA 4','Expenses7000','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1537','Pull-up Isometric Hold','Pull-up Isometric Hold','Hold the pull-up movement in any position','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS','BACK']::text[],ARRAY['biceps','lats']::text[],ARRAY['PULL_UP_BAR']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1537','CC-BY-SA 4','DiscoCop','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1545','Archer Pull Up','Archer Pull Up','An archer pull-up is an advanced bodyweight exercise that strengthens your back and biceps, serving as a progression towards a one-arm pull-up. It gets its name from the "bow-drawing" position your body takes at the top.Here''s how to do it:1. Grip: Grasp a pull-up bar with a wider-than-shoulder-width overhand grip.2. Starting Position: Hang with arms fully extended, engaging your core and keeping your shoulder blades pulled down.3. The Pull: Pull your body up towards one hand, similar to a regular pull-up. At the same time, extend the other arm out to the side, keeping it as straight as possible. Your chin should come towards the hand that is pulling.4. Hold & Lower: Briefly hold the top position where one arm is bent and pulling, and the other is extended. Slowly lower yourself back to the starting position with control.5. Alternate: Repeat the movement, pulling up towards the opposite hand.The key to this exercise is to keep the assisting arm as straight as possible to maximise the load on the working arm. This makes it a challenging but effective exercise for unilateral pulling strength.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['lats','biceps']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1545','CC-BY-SA 4','Antsy6277','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1546','Larsen Press','Larsen Press','Put your legs up on a separate Bench and press with no leg drive','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['chest','triceps']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1546','CC-BY-SA 4','Expenses7000','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1548','Stair Master','Stair Master','cardio and lower-body strength machine designed to simulate climbing stairs. It provides a low-impact, high-intensity workout that targets the legs, glutes, and core while improving cardiovascular endurance.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','calves','glutes','quads']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1548','CC-BY-SA 4','MisterPinnacle','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1551','Push-Up','Push-Up','The push-up is a fundamental bodyweight exercise that targets the chest, arms, and shoulders while engaging the core for stability. It requires no equipment and is excellent for building upper body strength.

Instructions:

Start in a plank position with hands placed slightly wider than shoulder-width, feet together, and body in a straight line from head to heels.
Engage your core and lower your body by bending your elbows, keeping them close to your body or flared slightly outward.
Lower until your chest nearly touches the ground while maintaining a neutral spine.
Push through your palms to return to the starting position, fully extending your arms.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST','SHOULDERS','CORE','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['chest','shoulders','core','triceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1551/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1551','CC-BY-SA 4','Settebello','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1554','Clap Push-UP','Clap Push-UP','The clap push-up is an explosive upper body movement that builds power and fast-twitch muscle strength. It adds a plyometric challenge to the traditional push-up by requiring the hands to leave the ground mid-rep.

Start in a strong push-up position with your hands slightly wider than shoulder-width and core engaged
Lower your body explosively and push off the ground with enough force to lift your hands.
Quickly clap your hands together at chest level before returning them to the floor.
Land with soft elbows to absorb the impact and immediately move into the next rep','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST','SHOULDERS','CORE','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['chest','shoulders','core','triceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1554/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1554','CC-BY-SA 4','Settebello','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1556','Devil’s Press','Devil’s Press','The Devil’s Press is a hybrid movement combining a dumbbell burpee and a double dumbbell snatch. It’s a full-body, high-intensity exercise that develops strength, power, and metabolic conditioning.

Start with a dumbbell in each hand and perform a burpee, letting your chest touch the ground while holding the dumbbells.
Explosively jump your feet forward and swing the dumbbells between your legs.
Drive the dumbbells overhead in one continuous motion, locking out your arms.
Lower the dumbbells with control to return to the starting position and repeat','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS','LEGS','CHEST','BACK','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders','glutes','chest','hamstrings','lats','triceps']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1556/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1556','CC-BY-SA 4','Settebello','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1567','Alternating dumbbell hammer curl','Alternating dumbbell hammer curl','Stand with your knees slightly bent and your back straight. Hold a dumbbell in each hand, using a neutral grip at your sides.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['biceps']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Arms','alternating',NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1567/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1567','CC-BY-SA 4','Mariano_O','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1572','Bird Dog','Bird Dog','The Bird Dog is a core stability exercise that enhances balance, coordination, and spinal alignment. It’s a low-impact movement ideal for strengthening the posterior chain and improving overall functional control.

Begin on all fours with hands under shoulders and knees under hips, keeping your spine neutral.
Extend your right arm forward and left leg backward simultaneously, keeping hips square.
Pause briefly at full extension while engaging your core and glutes.
Return to the starting position and repeat on the opposite side','CORE',ARRAY['CORE','SHOULDERS','LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['core','shoulders','glutes']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1572/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1572','CC-BY-SA 4','Settebello','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1573','Ab wheel','Ab wheel','Starting Position: Kneel on the floor with the ab wheel in front of you.
Grip the Wheel: Hold the handles firmly.
Roll Out: Slowly roll the wheel forward, extending your body while keeping your core tight.
Return: Pull the wheel back towards your knees, maintaining tension in your core.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],ARRAY['core']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1573/0.jpg',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1573','CC-BY-SA 4','lhegedus','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1574','Schwimmen','Schwimmen','800m Schwimmen in 20 Minuten','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],'{}'::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1574','CC-BY-SA 4','delta@romeo','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1575','Standing Dowel Shoulder press','Standing Dowel Shoulder press','Mobility - prone dowel press or standing dowel shoulder press alternating between front andback','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1575','CC-BY-SA 4','mountain.potato','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1576','Snap Down','Snap Down','Begin in a standing position with your
arms up over your head and your toes pushing into the ground raising
your heels up becoming as tall as you can. From this position, perform a
small hop with both feet slightly coming off of the ground. As you
land, bend your knees and begin to hinge forward at your hips absorbing
the landing. Return to the starting position and repeat. 
You should feel the muscles in your lower body working. 
Start with your arms and knees fully
straightened out. Keep a stable balance as you land and briefly hold
that end position, don’t go too fast and become off balanced. Keep your
chest up. 
For a detailed video on landing mechanics, click here: https://youtu.be/RThUCYRDyZw','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes','quads','calves','core']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1576','CC-BY-SA 4','mountain.potato','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1577','Bretzel stretch','Bretzel stretch','How to Perform the Bretzel Stretch    Starting Position:        Lie on your back on a flat surface, such as a mat.        Bend your knees and place your feet flat on the ground.    Leg Positioning:        Lift your right leg and cross it over your left leg, placing your right foot on the outside of your left knee.        Your left leg should remain flat on the ground.    Arm Positioning:        Extend your left arm out to the side at shoulder height, keeping it straight.        Use your right hand to gently pull your right knee towards the floor on the left side of your body.    Stretching:        As you pull your knee down, try to keep your left shoulder flat on the ground.        You should feel a stretch in your hip, lower back, and possibly your chest.    Hold the Position:        Maintain this position for 20-30 seconds, breathing deeply and relaxing into the stretch.        Switch sides and repeat the process.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','BACK','CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['glutes','hamstrings','lats','chest']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1577','CC-BY-SA 4','mountain.potato','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1578','Hip hinge','Hip hinge','Hip Hinge Exercise DescriptionThe hip hinge is a fundamental movement pattern that involves bending at the hips while keeping the spine neutral. It is commonly used in exercises like deadlifts, kettlebell swings, and good mornings. To perform a hip hinge:    Starting Position: Stand with your feet hip-width apart and a slight bend in your knees.    Hinge at the Hips: Push your hips back while maintaining a straight back. Your torso should lean forward, and your chest should stay up.    Lowering Phase: Continue to hinge until your torso is nearly parallel to the ground, or until you feel a stretch in your hamstrings.    Return to Standing: Drive through your heels and thrust your hips forward to return to the starting position.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes','calves','quads','core']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1578','CC-BY-SA 4','mountain.potato','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1579','Bronco','Bronco','Finess test for rugby.5 x shuttle runs of 20m, 40m, and 60m (20yd, 45yd, 65yd/far 45yd mark)
Complete as fast as possible','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1579','CC-BY-SA 4','MU5H','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_158','Close-grip Lat Pull Down','Close-grip Lat Pull Down','Grip the pull-down bar with your hands closer than shoulder width apart, with your palms facing away from you. Lean back slightly. Pull the bar down towards your chest, keeping your elbows close to your sides as you come down. Pull your shoulders back at the end of the motion.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_158/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','158','CC-BY-SA 3','tuckerm','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1580','Perpendicular Unilateral Landmine Row','Perpendicular Unilateral Landmine Row','Using a landmine attachment with the barbell running at 90 degrees to either side, hinge slightly at the hips and grip the barbell by the plate sleeve. Raise the barbell to chest height, with your upper arm parallel to the floor at the top of the movement.','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Shoulders','unilateral',NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1580','CC-BY-SA 4','CptnFatbeard','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1581','Trap press','Trap press','Laying flat with arm pointing straight upwards, elbow fully extended, use only your shoulder to raise the weight as high as comfortable and back to rest.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL','BENCH','DUMBBELL']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1581/0.jpg',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1581','CC-BY-SA 4','CptnFatbeard','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1582','Side-laying interior rotation','Side-laying interior rotation','Laying on your side and using the arm closest to the bench, maintain a 90 degree bend at the elbow and allow the weight to fall towards the ground past the bench as low as comfortable. Bring the weight up using only rotation of the shoulder to the opposing shoulder.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL','BENCH','DUMBBELL']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1582/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1582','CC-BY-SA 4','CptnFatbeard','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1583','Supine press','Supine press','Take a close grip and push perpendicular to the bench. Keep back flat.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL','BENCH']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1583','CC-BY-SA 4','CptnFatbeard','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1584','March or jog in place','March or jog in place','Low-impact cardiovascular exercise that simulates running without moving, ideal for warming up, improving endurance, or training in small spaces.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','SHOULDERS','ARMS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','calves','glutes','quads','shoulders','biceps','core','triceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1584','CC-BY-SA 4','MrAlfaRobot','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1589','Leg and hip stretch','Leg and hip stretch','Gentle movements to improve the flexibility of the lower body and release tension in the hips.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS','BACK']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes','quads','lats']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1589','CC-BY-SA 4','MrAlfaRobot','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1590','Arm and neck stretch','Arm and neck stretch','Relieves tension in the upper body, especially useful for people with office jobs or prolonged postures.','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS','ARMS','BACK']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders','triceps','lats']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1590','CC-BY-SA 4','MrAlfaRobot','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1591','Deep breathing (standing or seated)','Deep breathing (standing or seated)','A conscious breathing technique to improve oxygenation, reduce stress and connect with the present moment.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],ARRAY['core']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1591','CC-BY-SA 4','MrAlfaRobot','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1592','Guided or free meditation','Guided or free meditation','A mental mindfulness exercise that helps reduce anxiety, improve focus and enhance overall well-being.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1592','CC-BY-SA 4','MrAlfaRobot','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1593','Smith Machine Split Squat','Smith Machine Split Squat','The "Smith Machine Split Squat" is a strength exercise
performed on the Smith machine, ideal for targeting the legs and glutes.
It involves placing one foot forward and the other back, lowering the
body until the front thigh is parallel to the ground while the bar
slides vertically along the machine. This exercise helps improve
stability, correct muscle imbalances, and reduces stress on the back,
providing a safe environment for strength training.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','quads']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1593/0.gif',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1593','CC-BY-SA 4','workout@rooven.anonaddy.me','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1602','Dumbbell Scaption','Dumbbell Scaption','Starting position:

Stand upright with your feet shoulder-width apart.

Hold a dumbbell in each hand with a neutral grip (palms facing each other).

Keep a slight bend in your elbows, shoulders relaxed and shoulder blades pulled back.

Execution:

Slowly raise the dumbbells forward and slightly to the side, at about a 30–45° angle from your body (between a front raise and a lateral raise).

Lift until your arms are about shoulder height (do not go overhead).

Pause for a second at the top and feel the activation in your shoulders and scapular stabilizers.

Lower the dumbbells back down under control to the starting position.

Breathing:

Inhale as you lower the dumbbells.

Exhale as you lift them.

Common mistakes:

Using dumbbells that are too heavy (causing jerky movements).

Lifting above shoulder height (puts unnecessary stress on the shoulder joint).

Arching the lower back instead of keeping the movement controlled at the shoulders.

Purpose of the exercise:

Activates the lower trapezius, serratus anterior, and scapular stabilizers.

Helps correct posture and improve shoulder balance.','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1602','CC-BY-SA 4','zdelko','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1603','Leg curl with elastic','Leg curl with elastic','Standing position: Place the band under your feet in the middle of your foot and grasp it with a hammer grip.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['calves','hamstrings']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Calves',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1603','CC-BY-SA 4','clafal','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1604','Sliding Lateral Lunge','Sliding Lateral Lunge','Stand upright with your feet hip-width apart, holding a kettlebell close to your chest in goblet position. Place one foot on a slider, gliding disc, or towel. Keep your chest tall and core engaged. Slowly slide the foot outward to the side while bending the opposite knee, lowering your hips into a lateral lunge. Keep the working leg’s knee aligned with the toes and avoid letting it collapse inward. The non-working leg remains straight and slides smoothly along the floor. Push through the heel of the bent leg to return to the starting position while maintaining control of the kettlebell.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes','quads','calves']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1604/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1604','CC-BY-SA 4','RiccaBaro','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1605','Copenhagen Adduction Exercise','Copenhagen Adduction Exercise','Lie on your side with the elbow placed directly under the shoulder, similar to a side plank position. Place the upper leg on a bench with the inside of the foot or ankle resting on it. Lift your hips and hold your body in a straight line while keeping the lower leg off the floor. The exercise strongly activates the adductors of the upper leg while also challenging the core and hip stabilizers.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['glutes','quads']::text[],ARRAY['BENCH']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1605','CC-BY-SA 4','RiccaBaro','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1606','Arm Raises (T/Y/I)','Arm Raises (T/Y/I)','Stand upright with feet hip-width apart, core engaged, and arms relaxed at your sides. From this position, perform three different arm raises to target the shoulders and upper back:

T Raise: Lift your arms straight out to the sides until they are parallel to the floor, forming a “T” shape.
Y Raise: Lift your arms upward at about a 45° angle from your body to form a “Y” shape.
I Raise: Raise your arms straight overhead, close together, forming an “I” shape.

Maintain a neutral spine, avoid arching your lower back, and move slowly with control. Focus on squeezing your shoulder blades together and keeping your shoulders down away from your ears.','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS','BACK']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders','lats']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1606','CC-BY-SA 4','WiNNiE','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1607','Typewriter Pull-ups','Typewriter Pull-ups','Hang from a pull-up bar with an overhand grip, slightly wider than shoulder-width. Pull yourself up until your chin is above the bar. At the top position, instead of going straight down, shift your body to one side by extending one arm while keeping the other arm bent. Move smoothly from one side to the other, like a typewriter motion, before lowering yourself back down. This exercise increases time under tension and strengthens the lats, biceps, and shoulders while building unilateral pulling strength.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS','BACK']::text[],ARRAY['biceps','lats']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1607','CC-BY-SA 4','tenebrizz','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1608','Bodyweight Biceps Curl','Bodyweight Biceps Curl','Set up under a straight bar (around waist or chest height). Grip the bar with your palms facing you (supinated grip), hands about shoulder-width apart. Lean back with straight arms so that your body is at an angle to the ground. Keeping your elbows high and close to the bar, pull your upper body toward the bar by flexing your elbows, similar to a biceps curl. Lower yourself back down in a controlled manner. Keep your core tight and movement slow to maximize tension on the biceps.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['biceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1608','CC-BY-SA 4','tenebrizz','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_161','Cross-Bench Dumbbell Pullovers','Cross-Bench Dumbbell Pullovers','Grasp a moderately weighted dumbbell so your palms are flat against the underside of the top plates and your thumbs are around the bar. Lie on your back across a flat bench so only your upper back and shoulders are in contact with the bench. Your feet should be set about shoulder-width apart and your head should hang slightly downward. With the dumbbell supported at arm''s length directly about your chest, bend your arms about 15 degrees and keep them bent throughout the movement. Slowly lower the dumbbell backward and downward in a semicircle arc to as low a position as is comfortably possible. Raise it slowly back along the same arc to the starting point, and repeat for the required number of repetitions.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK','CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['lats','chest']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_161/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','161','CC-BY-SA 3','powerade69','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1612','kettlebell sumo deadlift','kettlebell sumo deadlift','Place your feet wider than shoulder-width apart, pointing your toes outward.
Keep your back straight, shoulders back, and chest up.
The knees must follow the same direction as the toes and not move forward.
When going up, it is advisable to contract your glutes to maximize the effectiveness of the exercise..','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes','quads']::text[],ARRAY['KETTLEBELL']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1612/0.jpg',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1612','CC-BY-SA 4','Tierrasverdes','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1613','rubber band glute kickback','rubber band glute kickback','A rubber band glute kickback is an exercise performed on hands and knees, where you anchor a resistance band to your foot and kick your heel back and up, squeezing your glute at the top of the movement, while maintaining a stable core and flat back to target and strengthen the gluteal muscles.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['glutes']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1613/0.jpg',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1613','CC-BY-SA 4','Tierrasverdes','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1615','Treadmill Cardio','Treadmill Cardio','It''s a cardiovascular workout performed on a treadmill to improve endurance, burn calories, and promote heart health. It offers multiple benefits, such as the ability to easily adjust speed and incline, allowing for a variety of workouts, from hill walking to high-intensity interval training (HIIT) and tempo runs. To optimize results, it''s important to vary your workouts.
Benefits of Treadmill Cardio:

Improved Cardiovascular Health:
Regular aerobic exercise on a treadmill strengthens the heart and lungs, which can help prevent heart disease.
Weight Loss:
By burning calories, treadmill training can be an effective tool for weight loss and maintaining a healthy weight.
Muscle Strengthening:
It strengthens the muscles in the legs and glutes, helping to prevent muscle loss, which is especially important with age.
Training Flexibility:
Allows you to vary speed, incline, and intervals, making your workout more dynamic and adaptable to different fitness levels.
Types of Treadmill Training:
Hill Walking:
Increasing the incline of the treadmill and walking at a brisk pace can improve endurance and work your leg muscles.
Endurance Jog:
A run at a comfortable, steady pace that keeps your heart rate in a target zone (such as Zone 2) to build endurance.
Interval Training:
Alternating periods of high intensity (fast running) with periods of low intensity (easy walking or jogging) to maximize calorie burn and cardiovascular improvement.
Tempo Run:
A run at a steady, sustained pace that is slightly faster than a normal jog, which challenges your ability to sustain a more intense effort for an extended period.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1615/0.jpg',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1615','CC-BY-SA 4','Tierrasverdes','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1616','Dumbbell donkey kick','Dumbbell donkey kick','The dumbbell donkey kick is an effective exercise for working the glute and hamstring muscles. This exercise is usually performed on all fours, using a dumbbell to add resistance. The steps for performing it correctly are described below.
Starting position: Get on all fours on a mat, with your hands aligned under your shoulders and your knees aligned under your hips.
Placing the dumbbell: Take a dumbbell and place it behind the knee of the leg you are going to lift. Make sure it is held securely to prevent it from falling during the exercise.
Exercise:
Inhale and, as you exhale, lift the leg with the dumbbell upward, keeping the knee bent at a 90-degree angle.
Raise the leg until it is parallel to the floor, feeling the contraction in the glutes.
Hold the position for one second at the top and then slowly lower the leg back to the starting position.
Repetitions: Perform 10 to 15 repetitions per leg, making sure to maintain good form throughout the exercise.
Sets: Complete 2 to 3 sets, depending on your fitness level.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1616/0.jpg',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1616','CC-BY-SA 4','Tierrasverdes','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1618','Stationary bike cardio','Stationary bike cardio','Benefits of Stationary Bike Cardio
Stationary bike cardio is an excellent form of exercise that offers multiple benefits for health and well-being. Here are some of the most notable ones:
Improved Cardiovascular Health
Strengthens the heart: Regular exercise helps improve the heart''s efficiency and reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.
Increases circulation: It improves blood flow, which can help lower blood pressure.
Calorie Burning and Weight Loss
Efficient for burning calories: Depending on the intensity, you can burn between 400 and 600 calories per hour.
Helps with weight loss: Combined with a balanced diet, stationary bike cardio can be effective for losing weight.
Muscle Strengthening
Tones the legs: It mainly works the leg muscles, including the quadriceps, hamstrings and calves.
Improves muscular endurance: Over time, you can increase the endurance and strength in your legs.
Mental Health Benefits
Reduces stress: Exercise releases endorphins, which can improve your mood and reduce stress.
Boosts energy: Regular physical activity can raise your energy levels and improve sleep quality.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1618/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1618','CC-BY-SA 4','Tierrasverdes','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1620','Seated Dumbbell Calf Raise','Seated Dumbbell Calf Raise','The seated dumbbell calf raise is a targeted exercise for strengthening the lower leg muscles. It follows the same movement pattern as the machine version: you place the weight on your knees, and by extending and flexing your ankles, you move the weight.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['calves']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Calves',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1620/0.jpeg',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1620','CC-BY-SA 4','AlucardEvil40','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
