('wger_1689','Cable Fly Middle Chest','Cable Fly Middle Chest','Execution

Start position: Arms extended out to your sides (a big “T” shape), feeling a stretch in your chest.

Movement: Bring your hands together in front of your chest in a wide hugging motion.

Focus on squeezing your chest at the center.
Do not lock your elbows or turn it into a press.

Return: Slowly open your arms back to the start, maintaining tension on the chest.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['chest']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1689','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1690','Cable Fly Upper Chest','Cable Fly Upper Chest','Execution

Start position:

Arms down and slightly behind your body, elbows slightly bent.
Feel a light stretch across your chest.

Movement:

Bring your hands upward and together in front of your upper chest — roughly at chin to collarbone level.
Use a smooth, controlled motion (avoid jerking).
Squeeze your chest at the top for 1–2 seconds.

Return:

Slowly let your arms move back down and out to the sides, keeping control and tension.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['chest']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1690','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1691','Cable Fly Lower Chest','Cable Fly Lower Chest','Execution

Start position:

Arms extended out to your sides in a “Y” shape.
Feel a stretch in your chest (slight bend in elbows — don’t lock them).

Movement:

Bring your hands downward and together in front of your lower chest or upper abs, in a smooth arc motion.
Squeeze your chest hard at the bottom for 1–2 seconds.

Return:

Slowly raise your arms back up along the same path until you feel a good stretch in your chest.
Maintain control — don’t let the weights pull you back too quickly.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['chest']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1691','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1693','Incline Static Hold','Incline Static Hold','Execution

Hold Position:

Lower the dumbbells a few inches above your chest — about the midpoint of a normal incline press.
This is where your upper chest is under maximum tension.

Static Hold:

Hold the position for 20–45 seconds while keeping the chest tight.
Breathe slowly but stay tense — do not relax your chest or shoulders.

End the set:

After the hold, carefully bring the dumbbells down and rest.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['chest']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1693','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1694','Flat Machine Press','Flat Machine Press','Execution

Start position:

Elbows bent at roughly 90°, hands just outside your chest.
Keep wrists straight and aligned with your forearms.

Pressing phase (concentric):

Push the handles forward until your arms are almost fully extended — don’t lock out.
Focus on squeezing your chest at the end of the movement.

Returning phase (eccentric):

Slowly bring the handles back toward your chest, feeling the stretch in your pecs.
Maintain tension — don’t let the weight stack touch down between reps.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['chest']::text[],ARRAY['BENCH']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1694','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1695','Pull-Ups (Wide Grip)','Pull-Ups (Wide Grip)','Execution

Pulling phase (concentric):

Drive your elbows down and slightly back, pulling your chest toward the bar.
Focus on leading with your chest, not your chin.
Keep your shoulders depressed (avoid shrugging).

Top position:

Chin should clear the bar (or at least reach bar level).
Pause for a brief squeeze in your lats.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1695','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1696','Pull-Ups (Neutral Grip)','Pull-Ups (Neutral Grip)','Execution

Pulling phase (concentric):

Drive your elbows down and back, pulling your chest toward your hands.
Keep your shoulders depressed and retracted (avoid shrugging).
Focus on squeezing your lats and upper back at the top.

Top position:

Chin should clear or reach the level of your hands.
Pause briefly and contract your lats hard.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1696','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1698','Barbell Row (Overhand)','Barbell Row (Overhand)','Execution

Starting position:

Hold the barbell with arms fully extended toward the floor.
Maintain tension in your lats and back muscles.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1698','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1699','Barbell Row (Underhand)','Barbell Row (Underhand)','Execution

Starting position:

Hold the barbell with arms fully extended toward the floor.
Maintain tension in your lats and back muscles.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1699','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1700','Barbell Romanian Deadlift (RDL)','Barbell Romanian Deadlift (RDL)','Execution

Hinge at the hips:

Push your hips backward, keeping a slight bend in the knees (soft knees).
Lower the barbell along the front of your thighs/shins while maintaining a neutral spine.
Keep the bar close to your body.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes']::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1700','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1701','One-Arm Heavy Row','One-Arm Heavy Row','Execution

Rowing phase (concentric):

Pull the dumbbell toward your lower chest or waist, leading with your elbow.
Keep your torso stable — avoid twisting or rotating your shoulders.
Pause at the top and squeeze your lats.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Back','unilateral',NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1701','CC-BY-SA 4','daiben','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1702','Jalón al pecho con agarre ancho','Jalón al pecho con agarre ancho','The lat pulldown is a pulling exercise that primarily targets the latissimus dorsi muscles (commonly known as “lats”) in your back. It involves pulling a cable bar or handle down towards your chest while seated on a machine specifically designed for this exercise. The lat pulldown is typically performed with a wide grip, but can also be done with a narrow grip or underhand grip to target different muscle groups in the back and arms. It is a popular exercise for building upper body strength and improving posture.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['lats','biceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1702','CC-BY-SA 4','Mariano_O','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1703','Patadas traseras','Patadas traseras','The dumbbell kickback is a popular strength training exercise that targets the triceps muscles in the back of your upper arms. It helps strengthen and tone the triceps, contributing to overall arm strength and aesthetics.

Among the exercises that work the arm muscles, kickback and its variations are very effective. You can easily apply these exercises with dumbbells, cables or resistance bands.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['triceps']::text[],ARRAY['BENCH','DUMBBELL']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1703','CC-BY-SA 4','Mariano_O','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1704','Tuck L-sit','Tuck L-sit','The tuck L-sit is a bodyweight hold where you support yourself on parallel bars, parallettes, or the floor with straight arms while pulling your knees toward your chest. Your hips stay lifted, your spine stays tall, and your feet hover off the ground. The goal is to keep your core tight, shoulders depressed, and arms locked out while maintaining the tucked position. It’s a core-intensive, shoulder-stabilizing static hold often used as a progression toward the full L-sit.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE','CHEST','LEGS','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['core','chest','quads','triceps']::text[],ARRAY['BENCH']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1704','CC-BY-SA 4','clafal','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1706','Bulgarian Squat with Dumbbells','Bulgarian Squat with Dumbbells','The Bulgarian split squat consists of performing a squat on one leg with the rear foot resting on a raised platform. Elevating your back leg on a bench creates instability and increases the range of motion of the exercise.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['glutes','quads','hamstrings']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1706/0.jfif',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1706','CC-BY-SA 4','AlucardEvil40','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1707','Elevación lateral polea','Elevación lateral polea','Lateral elevation unilateral using a polea','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1707','CC-BY-SA 4','polloperro','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1709','Reverse Fly Standing','Reverse Fly Standing','Stand with a good posture holding a cable in each hand opposite the machine
Starting with the hands in front of your chest pull the arms out to
your side into the shape of a T with the elbows nearly straight
Slowly release the arms forward to the start
Repeat with the arms moving into the shape of a Y
Repeat with the arms in the shape of a W
Repeat with the arms in the shape of an L by your side','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1709','CC-BY-SA 4','Croak6728','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_171','Incline Crunches','Incline Crunches','Lay down on your back on a inclined bench, feet are on one end of the bench. Ask a partner or use some other help (barbell, etc.) to keep them fixed, your hands are behind your head. From this position move your upper body up till your head or elbows touch your knees. Do this movement by rolling up your back.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],ARRAY['core']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_171/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','171','CC-BY-SA 3','wger.de','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1710','Butchers Block Stretch','Butchers Block Stretch','Kneel down with the hands together
Rest the elbows on a bench in front of you and lower the chest down
Keep the elbows bent with good posture and hold','BACK',ARRAY['BACK']::text[],ARRAY['lats']::text[],ARRAY['BENCH']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1710','CC-BY-SA 4','Croak6728','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1711','Sleeper Stretch','Sleeper Stretch','Lie on your side so that you are resting a little weight on your shoulder blade to keep it still
Extend your arm to 90 degrees in front of you, resting on the floor/mat
Lift your hand with your elbow so that your fingers point to the ceiling and your palm points towards your feet
Keeping your elbow still, gently lower your palm toward the floor','SHOULDERS',ARRAY['SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['shoulders']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1711','CC-BY-SA 4','Croak6728','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1712','Horizontal Shoulder Flexion Stretch','Horizontal Shoulder Flexion Stretch','Raise your arm out to the side, placing your hand on a wall or doorway beside you at shoulder height
Turn your body away from your arm to stretch the chest, as far as is comfortable','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST','SHOULDERS']::text[],ARRAY['chest','shoulders']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1712','CC-BY-SA 4','Croak6728','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1713','Doorway Pectoral Stretch','Doorway Pectoral Stretch','Place the forearm vertically on a door frame or stationary object at head height
