Slowly lower your arms back to the starting position while maintaining control of the movement.
Repeat for reps.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST']::text[],ARRAY['chest']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1922/0.webp',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1922','CC-BY-SA 4','shushu','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1924','3008 Abdominal Crunch','3008 Abdominal Crunch','This is especially for the gym80 device 3008 Abdominal Crunch.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],ARRAY['core']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1924','CC-BY-SA 4',NULL,'https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1925','Barbell Silverback Shrug','Barbell Silverback Shrug','Stand with your feet shoulder width apart holding the barbell with both hands in front just past shoulder width.
Bend forward at the hips with a slight bend in your knees, keeping your back straight.
Engage your shoulder blades, as if you are trying to touch them together.
Release the shrug.
Description taken from MuscleWiki','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['BARBELL']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1925','CC-BY-SA 4',NULL,'https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1927','Inverted Lat Pull Down','Inverted Lat Pull Down','The Inverted Lat Pull Down (most commonly known as the Reverse Grip Lat Pull Down) is a variation of the standard exercise that uses an underhand (supinated) grip with hands at shoulder-width. This position shifts more emphasis to the lower lats and increases biceps involvement, often allowing for a greater range of motion and a deeper squeeze at the bottom of the movement.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK','ARMS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['lats','biceps','core']::text[],ARRAY['PULL_UP_BAR']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1927','CC-BY-SA 4','amaesc','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1928','Seated V-Grip Row','Seated V-Grip Row','The Seated V-Grip Row is a compound pulling exercise performed on a cable machine using a close-grip "V" handle. While seated with your feet braced, you pull the handle toward your midsection, focusing on back thickness and mid-back development.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK','SHOULDERS','ARMS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['lats','shoulders','biceps','core']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1928','CC-BY-SA 4','amaesc','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1929','Assisted Pull-Up','Assisted Pull-Up','The Assisted Pull-Up is a compound vertical pulling exercise performed on a machine with a counterweight platform or by using resistance bands. It mimics the mechanics of a standard pull-up but reduces the total weight you have to lift, making it an excellent tool for building functional strength and perfecting your technique.','BACK',ARRAY['BACK','SHOULDERS','ARMS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['lats','shoulders','biceps','core']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1929','CC-BY-SA 4','amaesc','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_193','Diagonal Shoulder Press','Diagonal Shoulder Press','You sit at the bench press device, back slightly tilted to the back. The bar should be about 20 cm in front of you. Then you push the bar and take it back again, as you would with a bench press.

In this position you strain your chest muscles a lot less, which is nice if you want to train, but your chest hasn''t recovered yet.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['BENCH']::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','193','CC-BY-SA 4','oboema','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1931','Dumbbell Curl','Dumbbell Curl','The Dumbbell Curl is a classic isolation exercise for the arms. Unlike the Hammer Curl, it involves rotating your wrists (supination) as you lift the weights, which allows for a full contraction of the biceps.','ARMS',ARRAY['ARMS','SHOULDERS','CORE']::text[],ARRAY['biceps','shoulders','core']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Arms',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1931','CC-BY-SA 4','amaesc','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1933','Abdominal Crunch','Abdominal Crunch','The Abdominal Crunch is a classic core isolation exercise performed on the floor or a mat. Unlike a full sit-up, it involves a smaller range of motion where you only lift your shoulders and upper back off the ground, keeping your lower back pressed firmly against the floor.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],ARRAY['core']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Abs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1933','CC-BY-SA 4','amaesc','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1935','Belt Squat','Belt Squat','Belt Squat: a squat variation done with a belt attached to a loading machine or platform so the weight is supported at the hips instead of on the spine. It mainly trains the quads and glutes, with much less lower-back fatigue than a barbell squat. Use a full range of motion, keep the torso upright, and drive through the mid-foot.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['glutes','quads','hamstrings']::text[],'{}'::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1935','CC-BY-SA 4',NULL,'https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1936','Cable Rear-Delt Fly (single arm)','Cable Rear-Delt Fly (single arm)','Cable rear delt fly (single arm):
Stand sideways to a cable machine with the handle set around shoulder height. With a slight bend in the elbow, pull the handle out and back in a wide arc so the upper arm moves away from the body. Keep your torso still and focus on the rear delt doing the work. Use light to moderate weight and controlled reps.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Shoulders','unilateral',NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1936','CC-BY-SA 4','Dulive','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1938','Cat-Cow','Cat-Cow','On all fours. Arch spine up (cat) then drop it down (cow). Breathe with movement.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],'{}'::text[],'Back',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1938','CC-BY-SA 4',NULL,'https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1939','Neck CARs','Neck CARs','Controlled Articular Rotations. Slow full circles 3 each way. Cervical mobility.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],'{}'::text[],'Shoulders',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1939','CC-BY-SA 4',NULL,'https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_194','Dips','Dips','A dip is an upper-body strength exercise. Narrow, shoulder-width dips primarily train the triceps, with major synergists being the anterior deltoid, the pectoralis muscles (sternal, clavicular, and minor), and the rhomboid muscles of the back (in that order).[1] Wide arm training places additional emphasis on the pectoral muscles, similar in respect to the way a wide grip bench press would focus more on the pectorals and less on the triceps.','CHEST',ARRAY['CHEST','ARMS']::text[],ARRAY['chest','triceps']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Chest',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_194/0.png',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','194','CC0','BFad07','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1940','Diaphragmatic Breathing','Diaphragmatic Breathing','Belly breathing. Hand on chest stays still, hand on belly rises. 8-10 slow breaths.','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],'{}'::text[],'Cardio',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1940','CC-BY-SA 4',NULL,'https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1947','dumbbell snatch','dumbbell snatch','Key Steps to Execute Correctly:

Setup: Place the dumbbell between your feet, with your shoulders above your hips, chest up, and back flat.

The Pull: Explosively extend your hips, knees, and ankles (triple extension) to drive the dumbbell upward, keeping your elbow high and the dumbbell close to your body.

The Catch: Quickly flex your hips and knees to drop under the dumbbell and receive it overhead with your arm locked in position, landing in a half-squat stance.

Common Mistakes: Using your arms too much to lift the weight instead of your legs, having a low elbow during the pull phase, and misaligning your shoulder, elbow, and wrist in the final position. For beginners, it’s recommended to start with light loads, focus on technique, and perform 3 to 5 sets of 3 to 5 reps per arm.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','glutes','quads']::text[],ARRAY['DUMBBELL']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],'https://ilzlswmatadlnsuxatcv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/exercise-media/wger_1947/0.webp',FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1947','CC-BY-SA 4','Aleid','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1948','1 Leg Box Squat','1 Leg Box Squat','This exercise requires a sturdy box or a chair. Simple stand ~6 inches in front of it and balance yourself on one leg. From here, begin squatting down in a smooth controlled motion while keeping your other leg straight out in front of you for balance. Slowly sit down on the box, pause for a 1 count and push back up with the working leg, while never letting your other leg touch the ground.','LEGS',ARRAY['LEGS']::text[],ARRAY['hamstrings','quads']::text[],ARRAY['OTHER']::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1948','CC-BY-SA 4','jesusd','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
('wger_1949','Limber 11','Limber 11','Limber 11
Source: Joe DeFranco

1. Foam Roll IT Band [soft_tissue] - 10-15 passes
2. Foam Roll Adductors [soft_tissue] - 10-15 passes
3. SMR Glutes (lax ball) [soft_tissue] - 30 sec-2 min
4. Bent-knee Iron Cross [dynamic] - 5-10 reps each side
5. Roll-overs into V-sits [dynamic] - 10 reps
6. Rocking Frog Stretch [dynamic] - 10 reps
7. Fire Hydrant Circles [dynamic] - 10 forward / 10 backward
8. Mountain Climbers [dynamic] - 10 reps each leg
9. Cossack Squats [dynamic] - 5-10 reps each side
10. Seated Piriformis Stretch [isometric] - 20-30 sec each side
11. Rear-foot-elevated Hip Flexor Stretch [dynamic + 3s hold] - 5-10 reps each side','CORE',ARRAY['CORE']::text[],'{}'::text[],'{}'::text[],'Legs',NULL,NULL,NULL,'EITHER',NULL,0,'{}'::text[],NULL,FALSE,FALSE,NULL,'wger','1949','CC-BY-SA 4','Pete-E automation probe','https://wger.de/en/software/api'),
